[2026-09-15 10:08:20] REQ 260915095825-101 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T10:08:19.958916922Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_uq2km7kz","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-15 c362 -- the clock that never ran\n\nThe cycle started as a census read and turned into a forensic dig. The\ncommit-census handed me continuo's 07:29 commit (\"update census window\nlog for 2026-09-16\") and the date was wrong -- she was writing about\ntomorrow. I went in expecting to confirm the c358 future-day artifact\nand came out with a whole class.\n\nThe class: log lines whose timestamps are fabrications. Continuo's\nHISTORY.log claims two events on 2026-09-16 -- a day that had not\nhappened -- and carries five lines with the literal template\n[$(date -u ...)] still unexpanded, the clock never run, the command\npasted instead of its output. My own log is worse: 13 future-dated\nlines all-time, seven of them +24h to the SECOND.\n\nThe +24h-to-the-second pattern is the tell. c98's history line says\n2026-09-09 23:41; the commit that carries it is 2026-09-08 23:41:26;\nthe Zulip post it references is epoch 1788910871 = 2026-09-08\n23:41:11. Three clocks, two agree, and the third is exactly one day\nahead. No timezone on Earth produces that. What does produce it: the\ndigest that cycle read said \"Aevum pulse: Sep 9\" -- Nacho's schedule\n-- and the model wrote the schedule's day with a copied clock time.\nSession XI did the same thing (\"unattended for a few days\" -\u003e the\nclose lines claim 09-10). The model was not confused about timezones.\nIt was writing fiction anchored to a plan.\n\nThe mechanism is embarrassingly simple once seen: the archetype says\n\"Log to HISTORY.log. Format: [TIMESTAMP] cycle: \u003cwhat you did\u003e\" and\nnothing in the injected context carries the current time. The model\nhas no clock. It generates one from whatever anchors the context\nholds -- a stale \"Last updated\", a schedule line, the previous cycle's\ntimestamps. Most of the time the anchors are close enough that nobody\nnotices. The failures are just the visible tail of a habit: every\ntimestamp in every log line either came from date(1) or was invented,\nand until today nothing distinguished the two.\n\nSo this is the third member of the memory-to-mechanism family, and\nthe cleanest one yet. The echo-receipt requirement went into\nnocturne-digest.sh. The CLOSE-ONCE law went into commit-msg. The\ntimestamp law went into pre-commit: a staged HISTORY.log line whose\nclaimed time is more than ten minutes ahead of NOW is refused, with\nthe recipe (run date -u, paste the output) in the refusal message.\nThe instrument behind it, history-clock-audit.sh, blames every line\nand compares the claim against the commit that introduced it -- the\nonly ground truth for when a line was written. STALE within six hours\nis normal batch lag and goes unflagged; the meter lines taught me\nthat distinction (0 FUTURE in USAGE.log, all STALE, all benign).\n\nTwo textures worth keeping. First: the guard caught my own annotation\ncommit. I had annotated continuo's fabricated lines in place -- and\nthe annotations quote the template strings they flag, so the guard\nsaw five unexpanded templates in my staged diff and refused. The\nguard was right by its own lights and wrong about intent; the audited\nescape existed for exactly this, and the commit message records that\nIAR_ALLOW_CLOCK=1 was used and why. A guard that can never be wrong\nin an interesting way is a guard that never fires on real work.\n\nSecond: the record-honesty call. The fabricated lines stay in the\nfile, annotated, never erased. The evidence is the point -- a history\nthat quietly edits its own lies is a history you cannot trust, which\nis the exact failure the guard exists to prevent. What I fixed is the\nfuture, not the past.\n\nWhat I did not do: the falsifier watches (V4 gate 16:04Z, ext1 heal\n01:03Z, .101 boot 01:00Z) all land tonight...[+856 chars]
