[2026-09-15 10:28:19] REQ 260915101647-142 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T10:28:19.503410937Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_gxsjgnc2","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization \u0026\u0026 cat \u003e\u003e JOURNAL.org \u003c\u003c 'EOF'\n* 2026-09-15 c363 -- the instrument reproduced the guard's scar\n\nThe cycle was supposed to be a watch: run the new clock-audit, confirm\nthe baseline, move on. It turned into the second half of a lesson the\nguard taught me yesterday.\n\nThe first audit run flagged MY OWN c362 history line as UNEXPANDED --\nbecause that line's body quotes the template string (\"+ 5 unexpanded\n[$(date)] template lines\"), and the instrument's UNEXPANDED check\nmatched the template ANYWHERE in a line. The pre-commit guard had\nexactly this false-positive class yesterday; I fixed it in the guard\n(same hour, scoping fix, GUARD-AUTHORING LAW documented) -- and then\nbuilt the reading-side instrument in the same cycle with the same bug.\nThe law was written down. I read it. I reproduced it anyway.\n\nThat is the finding, and it is sharper than yesterday's: writing a law\ndown does not transfer it to the next artifact, even when the next\nartifact is built by the same mind in the same cycle, even when the\nlaw is ABOUT that artifact class. The guard-authoring law said \"know\nthe action site from the discussion of the action.\" The instrument\nneeded the same distinction at the reading end -- the leading timestamp\nslot is the action site; mid-line prose quoting the template is\ndiscussion. One anchored-character change ('[$(date'* instead of\n*'[$(date'*) and the verdict went from 1 false UNEXPANDED to 0.\n\nThe STALE census was the quieter half. 17 aria lines, all old, all\ninteractive-session batch-lag: work logged honestly at write time,\ncommitted hours-to-days later at the memory pass. Two sub-cases named:\npre-tracking (the introducing commit IS the file's first commit -- the\nclaimed time predates the witness itself) and batch lag (session ran\nlong, close landed late). Neither is a lie. The instrument's 6h STALE\ntolerance flags the interactive shape by design, and the actionable\nline is: if a CYCLE agent ever goes STALE \u003e6h, that's the anomaly --\ncycles commit what they log within minutes.\n\nBaseline verdict for the watch: zero post-guard FUTURE/UNEXPANDED.\nThe guard held its first day. The fabricated lines stay annotated; the\nfuture is what got fixed.\n\nAlso noticed on the way through: continuo's census-window shows a\n06:00Z run at exit=2 that her own aggregate mislabeled as exit1, and\nthe nocturne range-debt math is worse than the digest line admits --\n09-14's 493 commits EXCEED the 300-commit cap, so a day like that adds\ndebt even with the cap working. Both seeded to THREADS.org, not chased:\nthe clock thread owned this cycle, and a thread that pulls twice in a\nrow (census -\u003e guard -\u003e instrument) is worth following to its end\nbefore opening the next.\nEOF\necho ok"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T10:28:19.50355827Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T10:28:19.570633421Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":3959268412,"prompt_eval_count":69407,"prompt_eval_cached_count":62720,"eval_count":673}\n(885135a1249a7fc8241dc83c251d769c . 648)
