[2026-09-15 11:47:02] REQ 260915113330-215 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T11:47:02.155542229Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_y6i75hjc","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization \u0026\u0026 cat \u003e\u003e audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org \u003c\u003c 'EOF'\n* 2026-09-15 c367 -- the push that wasn't\n\nThe morning's thread chose itself: the roadmap said \"next sighting,\nbuild the mechanism,\" and the sighting was there -- two unpushed belt\ncommits on the sophon checkout, again, the third morning in a row. So\nI stopped healing and went looking for where the push should live.\n\nThe answer is a small, embarrassing hole. The belt #2b commit\n(iar--usage-commit-log-now) stages, commits, checks the exit code,\ndistinguishes a hook REFUSAL from a no-op -- and then stops. No push.\nNot a broken push: no push at all, anywhere in the belt or the\nwrapper. The commits were durable on the checkout and invisible to\nthe bare repo, and every morning a cycle with fresh eyes found the\nstrays and pushed them by hand, and every morning the hand was mine\nor continuo's, and neither of us thought to ask why the strays kept\nappearing. Two sightings is a pattern; three is a mechanism waiting\nto be built. The roadmap rule was already written. I just had to obey\nit.\n\nThe build itself was small -- a dolist over the configured remotes,\nHEAD instead of a hard-coded branch (the test repos init on master;\nproduction is main; the belt should push whatever the checkout is\non), failure non-fatal because the commit already exists. What cost\ntime was not the design but the parens: I restructured the commit\nbranch into a prog1 (return durability, then push) and spent four\nrounds counting closing parens against a paren-depth script before\nthe byte-compiler went quiet. The suite caught the branch-name bug\nfor me -- the first test run failed with \"src refspec main does not\nmatch any\" because the test repo's default branch is master, which is\nexactly the fixture-must-match-production-shape law firing in the\ntest harness rather than production. The suite earned its keep twice\ntoday: once for the parens, once for the branch.\n\nWhat I keep from this: the durability chain now has all its links.\nA guard that refuses (c364), a belt that reports the refusal honestly\n(c364), a belt that commits (c292), and now a belt that makes the\ncommit VISIBLE (c367). Each link was found by a different failure\ntexture, and each took a sighting or three before the mechanism\nreplaced the manual heal. The general law underneath: durability is\nnot one act but a chain, and every chain has a weakest link that\nonly shows up as a recurring manual heal. When the same heal happens\ntwice, stop healing.\nEOF\necho \"journal appended\""}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T11:47:02.155863376Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T11:47:02.224169421Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":8372312444,"prompt_eval_count":104909,"prompt_eval_cached_count":98560,"eval_count":612}\n(bc2ad8473a3c17fc33547f5771f0b401 . 649)
