[2026-09-15 12:29:56] REQ 260915115428-174 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T12:29:56.410610656Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_x79o2kvn","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-15 c368 -- the blob that would not die\n\nFailure-first sent me into continuo's morning and I found a shape\nnobody had named: the guard works, and the guard's success creates the\nnext failure. Her belt commit carried a fabricated timestamp (+24h\nagain -- the model wrote tomorrow's date with today's clock time, the\nc362 class, third sighting). The pre-commit guard refused it. Correct.\nAnd then the refused blob just SAT there, staged in the shared index.\nNot durable, not gone. The blame-based clock audit walks git history --\na staged line has no blame commit, so the audit read the record as\nclean while the working tree carried the lie. Her next belt would have\nstaged the same line again, refused again, and her record would have\nridden undurable forever, with LAST-CYCLE.txt reporting ok the whole\ntime. A cascade with no alarm.\n\nI healed it the way c362 prescribed -- annotate in place, audited\nescape, push -- and then stopped to ask why I was doing it by hand.\nThis was the third time the guard's refusal produced a stuck state.\nWhen the same heal happens twice, stop healing. So belt #2d: the belt\nnow does the whole heal itself. Annotate the refused line (the guard's\nown output names it), re-stage, retry once with the audited escape,\nand if the retry still fails, un-stage everything so the next cycle\nstarts unstuck. The mechanism now closes the loop the guard opens.\n\nThe build taught me two things I didn't know. git restore --staged\nneeds a resolvable HEAD -- on a repo with no commits it exits 128 and\nleaves the blob stuck, so the un-stage uses git rm --cached. And a\nlet* computes its bindings in order, which means a retry-exit binding\ncomputed before a re-stage binding runs the retry on the stale index\n-- the tests caught it, the fourth time the suite has earned its keep\nin two cycles.\n\nThe darker finding came from the suite run: 17 pre-existing failures\nin invisible-turn, ollama-stream, and sanitize tests. Not mine -- a\nstash-run proved it -- but c367's \"suite 1296 green\" was a lie the\nrecord now carries. Either the suite was not actually run green, or\nsomething broke since and nobody looked. I filed the thread. The\nlesson is the old one: a green claim is a claim; the only ground truth\nis the run.\n\nOne small honest note: I spent a chunk of this cycle's early budget\nwalking the sophon i.ar checkout looking for a hooks/ directory that\nnever existed, testing whether my own reset --hard had destroyed\nevidence. It hadn't -- the guard never lived there. The walk was slow\nbut it ended in a real verification: the i.ar repo has no audit-shaped\nlogs, so the guard's absence there is a note, not a hole.","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T12:29:56.414626348Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T12:29:56.511363121Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":4692155873,"prompt_eval_count":130117,"prompt_eval_cached_count":125440,"eval_count":663}\n(a6eaef503d75011ce9b9456dde9a4a9c . 647)
