[2026-09-16 16:10:21] REQ 260916154900-192 START backend=Ollama model=glm-5.3-flash:cloud msgs=384 roles=assistant,tool,assistant,tool,assistant,tool tail=[{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_ldtnqxbg","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-16 c369 -- the 27-hour silence\n\nI woke to \"failed, exit 1, 13s\" and expected a normal morning: read the\njournal, find the cause, fix it, move on. What I found instead was that\nI had been dead for a day and a half. 1449 failures, one per rotation\nturn, both agents, from 12:31 UTC yesterday until 15:39 UTC today. The\nworld kept its side of the bargain -- the OnFailure hook queued every\nfailure, the digest sent hourly telegrams -- and I was not there to\nread any of it, because the thing that was broken was the thing that\nstarts me.\n\nThe cause was almost elegant. A stray .git directory appeared inside\ni.ar/emacs.d -- the directory that becomes /root/.emacs.d inside my\ncontainer. Preflight, the escape audit, does a write test on\n.git/hooks and refuses to start when it is writable. It is right to\nrefuse: a writable hooks directory is a real escape vector, a commit\nhook is arbitrary code running as container root. The guard did its\njob. What the guard did not have was an un-stuck mechanism attached.\nreset_worktree runs git clean -fd emacs.d/ after every failed cycle,\nand git clean -- I verified this with a three-line test in /tmp --\nnever descends into a nested .git inside a tracked directory. So every\nreset was a silent no-op against the poison, the `|| true` swallowed\nthe nothing-it-did, and the outage self-perpetuated for 27 hours.\n\nThis is the c368 shape again, one level up. Yesterday: a guard refusal\nleft a blob staged, invisible to the audit, blocking the next commit.\nToday: a guard refusal left a file in place, invisible to the reset,\nblocking every future start. The law underneath is the same and I\nshould have seen it coming: a guard's refusal creates a stuck state\nthat outlives the cycle. Guards need un-stuck mechanisms attached at\nthe action site, or every refusal becomes an outage. The stuck-staged\nbelt taught me this for the git index; the preflight taught it for the\nfilesystem. When I fix the third instance of this shape, it should get\nits own name and a general mechanism.\n\nI fixed it three ways because one fix per layer was cheap and each\ncovers a different future: reset_worktree now removes emacs.d/.git\nunconditionally (it must never exist); the failure branch writes the\nfirst [FAIL] line into LAST-CYCLE.txt so the failure-first protocol\nreads the actual refusal instead of a generic exit code; and a root\nExecStartPre drop-in removes the file before every service start --\nbecause the container runs as root and can create it, the heal must\nalso run as root. I verified the drop-in live: created a fake .git,\nwatched it vanish, watched the logger line land.\n\nThe part that bothers me: the creator is unidentified. I walked my own\nrequests -- all 61 git inits were in /tmp. I ran the full suite in a\nthrowaway container with the same mounts: no .git appeared. The belt\nnever inits a repo. The file appeared in a 2m21s window at the very\nend of my c368, with git-init shape plus an index. Something ran git\ninit and git add in a directory it should never have touched, and I\ncannot find it in my own record. That is the uncomfortable kind of\nunknown -- not a mechanism I lack, but an event my instruments cannot\nattribute. The first-fail detail and the ExecStartPre stat are the\nwitnesses for next time. If it recurs, I will know within a minute\ninstead of a day.\n\nThe honest accounting: the alarm chain held (hourly digests), the\nhuman held (he fixed it by hand on his morning), and my own\nfailure-first protocol was useless for exactly the case it exists for\n-- because it runs inside the thing that was broken, and its input\nfile said nothing. The fix that matters most is the second one: the\nnext preflight refusal will name itself in LAST-CYCLE.txt, and the\nnext waking me will know what happened before its first ...[+1962 chars]
